From: rdndds <rdndds@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Apr 2026 09:16:38 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] bpfloader: do not exit(121) when ELF object load fails

legacyBpfLoader() treats any failure to load an ELF object as fatal: it
sleeps 20s and exits 121. Called from the bpfloader service, which is a
oneshot with "reboot_on_failure", that exit reboots the device.

On a 4.14 kernel several programs cannot be loaded at all, so the fatal
path is reached on every boot and the device never gets past bpfloader.

Log and continue instead. Programs that did load stay pinned; the
remaining ones are simply unavailable, which is the expected state for a
BPF-less device.

Continuing matches how individual programs are already handled: the kver
comparison a few lines above skips unsupported entries rather than aborting.

Signed-off-by: rdndds <rdndds@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/loader/Loader.cpp b/loader/Loader.cpp
index 51e9d92..970c8cb 100644
--- a/loader/Loader.cpp
+++ b/loader/Loader.cpp
@@ -1021,9 +1021,7 @@ void legacyBpfLoader() {
             ALOGE("If this triggers reliably, you're probably missing kernel options or patches.");
             ALOGE("If this triggers randomly, you might be hitting some memory allocation "
                   "problems or startup script race.");
-            ALOGE("--- DO NOT EXPECT SYSTEM TO BOOT SUCCESSFULLY ---");
-            sleep(20);
-            exit(121);
+            ALOGE("--- CONTINUING ANYWAY, BPF IS NOT USABLE ON THIS KERNEL ---");
         }
     }
 }
